How to read this manual
***********************

This is a native GNU Info manual. Start at Top, select a lecture, and use its
section menu.

‘Tab’
     Move to the next menu entry or cross-reference.
‘Return’
     Follow the selected menu entry or cross-reference.
‘m’
     Choose a menu entry by name, for example ‘Git’ at Top.
‘n / p / u’
     Go to the next sibling, previous sibling, or parent node.
‘Space / Backspace’
     Read forward or backward through the manual, across section boundaries.
‘l’
     Return to the last visited node.
‘t’
     Return to Top.
‘g’
     Go to a named node, for example ‘Quality - Regular expressions’.
‘s / i’
     Search the manual text or concept index.
‘q’
     Quit Info.

Links
=====

Lecture and section references are native Info links. They work offline. Web
references keep their labels and complete URLs. Emacs Info and many terminal
emulators can open these URLs in a browser. The stand-alone GNU Info reader
does not provide native web browsing; copy a URL to your browser if your
terminal does not open it.

Code and diagrams
=================

Code blocks use literal, fixed-width text. Indentation, backslashes, prompts,
and long lines are preserved in the file. GNU Info wraps long lines on screen
and marks a continuation with a backslash. Use ‘C-x w’ to toggle display
wrapping, or widen your terminal. A line that begins with a shell or
interpreter prompt is a transcript; omit the prompt when you enter the command.
Illustrations have text labels and links. The Git history diagrams are retained
as text.

Scope
=====

The notes and exercises are complete for the pinned source snapshot. Source
comments hidden on the website are omitted. The added guides identify known
caveats without rewriting the original lesson or solving the exercises. Video
content is linked and has not been transcribed. Some live demonstrations exist
only in the recordings.


