1. Course Overview + Introduction to the Shell
**********************************************

Lecture date: 2026-01-12.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/course-shell/). Lecture
video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MSgoeuMqUmU).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

The shell connects small programs through arguments, streams, and exit status.
This lecture starts with paths and command discovery, then combines text tools
into pipelines. It ends with Bash control flow and exercises that turn commands
into reusable scripts. Read each example as a data flow: identify the input,
the operation, and the output before you run it.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Navigate with absolute and relative paths, and explain the current working
     directory.
   • Use documentation to inspect command options and locate programs.
   • Combine grep, sed, awk, sort, and uniq through pipes and redirections.
   • Read Bash variables, conditions, loops, and background jobs.

Reading notes
-------------

   • Console examples include a prompt and sample output. Enter only the
     command after the prompt. The displayed date is an example.
   • Bash can run builtins such as echo before it searches PATH. Use type or
     command -V to inspect command resolution; which alone can miss builtins
     and aliases.
   • The for-loop fragment is incomplete by design. Add its body and done
     before use. The stress example also needs the stress program and a
     suitable Rust project.
   • The sed -i form shown uses GNU syntax. Check your local sed manual before
     an in-place edit on another system.

