2. Command-line Environment
***************************

Lecture date: 2026-01-13.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/command-line-environment/).
Lecture video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ccBGsPedE9Q).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

Command-line tools share conventions that make them work together. This lecture
explains arguments, file streams, environment variables, exit status, and
signals. It then applies those concepts to SSH, tmux, shell configuration, and
terminal tools. Track which process owns each operation. In an SSH pipeline, a
change in quoting can move work between the local computer and the remote
computer.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Distinguish shell expansion from the arguments that a program receives.
   • Redirect standard input, standard output, and standard error separately.
   • Use exit status, signals, and job control to manage processes.
   • Configure remote connections and keep work in a tmux session.
   • Manage shell aliases and dotfiles with version control.

Reading notes
-------------

   • Quote argument expansions such as "$1" and "$@" when each value must
     remain one argument. The opening script is a teaching example: if $DEBUG
     executes its value as a command.
   • A shell script normally returns the status of its last command. An
     explicit exit selects another status. A nonzero grep status can mean no
     match or an error.
   • The ls-based deletion loop does not handle all filenames. Study expansion
     with harmless files before using a deletion command.
   • Terminal key sequences describe separate key presses. For tmux, press and
     release Ctrl-b, then press the next key.

Original lecture notes
======================

As we covered in the previous lecture, most shells are not a mere launcher to
start up other programs, but in practice they provide an entire programming
language full of common patterns and abstractions. However, unlike the majority
of programming languages, in shell scripting everything is designed around
running programs and getting them to communicate with each other simply and
efficiently.

In particular, shell scripting is tightly bound by _conventions_. For a command
line interface (CLI) program to play nicely within the broader shell
environment there are some common patterns that it needs to follow. We will now
cover many of the concepts required to understand how command line programs
work as well as ubiquitous conventions on how to use and configure them.

