3. Development Environment and Tools
************************************

Lecture date: 2026-01-14.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/development-environment/).
Lecture video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QnM1nVzrkx8).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

A development environment supports reading, navigation, edits, and checks. This
lecture combines three ideas: Vim treats editing as a small language; language
servers connect code meaning to editor features; AI tools propose code through
completion, inline edits, and agents. Start with a small working setup. Add
features when you can explain how they improve your normal work.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Change Vim modes and combine motions, operators, counts, and text objects.
   • Use code navigation, documentation, formatting, and type information in an
     editor.
   • Point the language server at the project environment and its dependencies.
   • Distinguish AI completion from inline edits and agent work.
   • Evaluate extensions for remote work, containers, and collaborative
     editing.

Reading notes
-------------

   • The Fizz Buzz program is deliberately broken. Preserve it while you follow
     the editing exercise. Key sequences assume the stated file layout and
     Normal mode.
   • AI completion examples show separate stages. Some blocks are function
     bodies or unfinished definitions, so they are not independent runnable
     programs.
   • The link-extraction expression demonstrates prompting. It does not parse
     every Markdown link form, including reference-style links used in this
     lecture.
   • The requests example omits a timeout and HTTP error check. Use a Markdown
     parser and explicit network error handling for a maintained downloader.

Original lecture notes
======================

A _development environment_ is a set of tools for developing software. At the
heart of a development environment is text editing functionality, along with
accompanying features such as syntax highlighting, type checking, code
formatting, and autocomplete. _Integrated development environments_ (IDEs) such
as VS Code (https://code.visualstudio.com/) bring together all of this
functionality into a single application. Terminal-based development workflows
combine tools such as tmux (https://github.com/tmux/tmux) (a terminal
multiplexer), Vim (https://www.vim.org/) (a text editor), Zsh
(https://www.zsh.org/) (a shell), and language-specific command-line tools,
such as Ruff (https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/) (a Python linter and code
formatter) and Mypy (https://mypy-lang.org/) (a Python type checker).

IDEs and terminal-based workflows each have their strengths and weaknesses. For
example, graphical IDEs can be easier to learn, and today's IDEs generally have
better out-of-the-box AI integrations like AI autocomplete; on the other hand,
terminal-based workflows are lightweight, and they may be your only option in
environments where you don't have a GUI or can't install software. We recommend
you develop basic familiarity with both and develop mastery of at least one. If
you don't already have a preferred IDE, we recommend starting with VS Code
(https://code.visualstudio.com/).

In this lecture, we'll cover:

   • *note Text editing and Vim: Development - Text editing and Vim.
   • *note Code intelligence and language servers: Development - Code
     intelligence and language servers.
   • *note AI-powered development: Development - AI-powered development.
   • *note Extensions and other IDE functionality: Development - Extensions and
     other IDE functionality.

