4. Debugging and Profiling
**************************

Lecture date: 2026-01-15.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/debugging-profiling/).
Lecture video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8VYT9TcUmKs).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

Use evidence to explain a failure or a performance limit. This lecture moves
from logs and breakpoints to execution recording, operating system tracing,
memory checks, and resource measurements. Select a tool for the question you
need to answer. Then reproduce the result before you change the program.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Choose logs, a debugger, or a system call trace to inspect program
     behavior.
   • Use a recorded execution to trace corrupted state back to its cause.
   • Distinguish elapsed time, CPU time, memory use, and sampled call stacks.
   • Measure a representative workload before and after an optimization.

Reading notes
-------------

   • The merge sort, memory corruption, and use-after-free exercises contain
     deliberate defects. Keep these defects until you use the specified tool to
     find them.
   • Replace placeholders such as <PID> with actual values. Angle brackets in a
     command are shell syntax, not part of a process identifier.
   • A flame graph groups stack samples. Its width shows the amount of sampled
     work; its horizontal position does not show when that work occurred.
   • Tracing tools have different operating system, hardware, and permission
     requirements. Read the linked tool documentation when an example cannot
     run on your system.

Original lecture notes
======================

A golden rule in programming is that code does not do what you expect it to do,
but what you tell it to do. Bridging that gap can sometimes be a quite
difficult feat. In this lecture we are going to cover useful techniques for
dealing with buggy and resource hungry code: debugging and profiling.

