6. Packaging and Shipping Code
******************************

Lecture date: 2026-01-20.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/shipping-code/). Lecture
video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KBMiB-8P4Ns).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

Shipping code requires an artifact and an explicit description of its
environment. This lecture uses Python packages to explain dependencies,
isolated environments, release versions, and reproducible builds. It then adds
containers, configuration, supporting services, and package registries. Track
what each step produces and what the next step needs.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Separate source code, build artifacts, installed packages, and running
     services.
   • Create a project environment and describe package metadata in
     pyproject.toml.
   • Distinguish compatible dependency ranges from a locked application
     environment.
   • Explain how images, containers, configuration, and Compose services fit
     together.

Reading notes
-------------

   • A fresh virtual environment does not contain requests. Install it in that
     environment before you run the lecture's import example. The displayed
     transcript omits this installation step.
   • An image tag such as python:3.12 or uv:latest can change. The improved
     Dockerfile demonstrates build caching but does not pin every input or
     switch to a non-root user.
   • Compose depends_on starts services in order. An application must still
     handle a dependency that is not ready to accept connections.
   • The systemd example starts Compose at boot. Its oneshot unit does not
     itself restart failed application containers; configure the container
     restart policy for that behavior.
   • Publishing examples include placeholder package URLs and version-specific
     output. Substitute your own artifact and registry details.

Original lecture notes
======================

Getting code to work as intended is hard; getting that same code to run on a
machine different from your own is often harder.

Shipping code means taking the code you wrote and converting it into a usable
form that someone else can run without your computer's exact setup. Shipping
code takes many forms and depends on the choices of programming language,
system libraries, and operating system, among many other factors. It also
depends on what you are building: a software library, a command line tool, and
a web service all have different requirements and deployment steps. Regardless,
there is a common pattern between all these scenarios: we need to define what
the deliverable is -- a.k.a. the _artifact_ -- and what assumptions it makes
about the environment around it.

In this lecture, we'll cover:

   • *note Dependencies & Environments: Shipping - Dependencies & Environments.
   • *note Artifacts & Packaging: Shipping - Artifacts & Packaging.
   • *note Releases & Versioning: Shipping - Releases & Versioning.
   • *note Reproducibility: Shipping - Reproducibility.
   • *note VMs & Containers: Shipping - VMs & Containers.
   • *note Configuration: Shipping - Configuration.
   • *note Services & Orchestration: Shipping - Services & Orchestration.
   • *note Publishing: Shipping - Publishing.

We'll explain these concepts through examples from the Python ecosystem, as
concrete examples are helpful for understanding. While the tools are different
for other programming language ecosystems, the concepts will largely be the
same.

