7. Agentic Coding
*****************

Lecture date: 2026-01-21.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/agentic-coding/). Lecture
video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTdz6PZoAnw).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

This lecture explains how a coding agent combines a language model,
conversation history, and tools. The main skill is to give the agent a clear
task and a useful feedback loop. You remain responsible for the result. Read
this lecture after the development environment lecture, then compare the
different ways an agent can help with the same small change.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Explain how a harness sends tool results and conversation history back to
     a model.
   • Give an agent a task that it can check with a compiler, linter, type
     checker, or test.
   • Choose between project instructions, skills, subagents, and separate
     worktrees.
   • Manage context and review the changes before you accept the result.

Reading notes
-------------

   • The opening Python program is a demonstration. Its regular expression
     finds a limited Markdown link form; it is not a complete Markdown parser.
   • Keep tool access, model inference, and the context window separate in your
     reasoning. A local command-line interface can still send data to a remote
     model.
   • Product features and model limits change. Treat the named tools and
     commands as the lecture's January 2026 examples.

Original lecture notes
======================

Coding agents are conversational AI models with access to tools such as
reading/writing files, web search, and invoking shell commands. They live
either in the IDE or in standalone command-line or GUI tools. Coding agents are
highly autonomous and powerful tools, enabling a wide variety of use cases.

This lecture builds on the AI-powered development material from the *note
Development Environment and Tools: Development. lecture. As a quick demo, let's
continue with the example from the *note AI-powered development: Development -
AI-powered development. section:

     from urllib.request import urlopen

     def download_contents(url: str) -> str:
         with urlopen(url) as response:
             return response.read().decode('utf-8')

     def extract(content: str) -> list[str]:
         import re
         pattern = r'\[.*?\]\((.*?)\)'
         return re.findall(pattern, content)

     print(extract(download_contents("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/missing-semester/missing-semester/refs/heads/master/_2026/development-environment.md")))

We can try prompting a coding agent with the following task:

     Turn this into a proper command-line program, with argparse for argument parsing. Add type annotations, and make sure the program passes type checking.

The agent will read the file to understand it, then make some edits, and
finally invoke the type checker to make sure the type annotations are correct.
If it makes a mistake such that it fails type checking, it will likely iterate,
though this is a simple task so that is unlikely to happen. Because coding
agents have access to tools that may be harmful, by default, agent harnesses
prompt the user to confirm tool calls.

     If the coding agent makes a mistake -- for example, if you have the ‘mypy’
     binary available directly on ‘$PATH’ but the agent tries calling ‘python
     -m mypy’ -- you can give it text feedback to help it course correct.
Coding agents support multi-turn interaction, so you can iterate on work over a
back-and-forth conversation with the agent. You can even interrupt the agent if
it's going down the wrong track. One helpful mental model might be that of a
manager of an intern: the intern will do the nitty gritty work, but will
require guidance, and will occasionally do the wrong thing and need to be
corrected.

     For a more illustrative demo, try asking the agent as a follow-up to run
     the resulting script. Observe the outputs, and try asking it to make a
     change (e.g., ask it to include only absolute URLs).

