9. Code Quality
***************

Lecture date: 2026-01-23.

Original notes (https://missing.csail.mit.edu/2026/code-quality/). Lecture
video (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XBiLUNx84CQ).

Study guide (added for this edition)
====================================

This lecture connects local code checks with repeatable project checks.
Formatters standardize syntax, linters detect selected problems, and tests
check observed behavior. Hooks and continuous integration run these tools at
useful points in development. The final sections introduce regular expressions
as a shared tool for search, replacement, and test selection. Work through a
small project so you can see both a passing check and an intentional failure.

Learning objectives
-------------------

   • Distinguish formatting, static analysis, tests, and coverage.
   • Configure checks that contributors can run locally and in continuous
     integration.
   • Explain the roles of pre-commit hooks, command runners, and deployment
     jobs.
   • Use regex groups and quantifiers, and recognize when a parser is the
     better tool.

Reading notes
-------------

   • Coverage shows which code ran. It does not show whether an assertion
     checked the correct result or whether every useful input was tested.
   • Regex syntax depends on the engine. The lecture uses Python syntax; grep
     and an editor can use different rules and replacement references.
   • In Python, a dot excludes a newline by default. Digit and word classes
     include Unicode characters unless an appropriate flag changes that
     behavior.
   • Read prompt examples and interpreter transcripts as examples; do not
     include their surrounding prose or prompt markers in a program.

Original lecture notes
======================

There are a variety of tools and techniques that support developers in writing
high-quality code. In this lecture, we'll cover:

   • *note Formatting: Quality - Formatting.
   • *note Linting: Quality - Linting.
   • *note Testing: Quality - Testing.
   • *note Pre-commit hooks: Quality - Pre-commit hooks.
   • *note Continuous integration: Quality - Continuous integration.
   • *note Command runners: Quality - Command runners.

As a bonus topic, we'll also cover *note regular expressions: Quality - Regular
expressions, a cross-cutting topic that has applications in code quality (e.g.,
for running a subset of tests that match a pattern) as well as other domains
like IDEs (e.g., for search and replace).

Many of these tools will be language-specific (e.g., the Ruff
(https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/) linter/formatter for Python). In some cases,
tools will support multiple languages (e.g., the Prettier
(https://prettier.io/) code formatter). The concepts, however, are near
universal -- you can find code formatters, linters, testing libraries, and so
on for any programming language.

